  '`trigonometry-and-measure-of-angles/lesson01.sa.html`, card 05. Old text: "$$1 \\text{ radian} = \\dfrac{\\text{arc length}}{\\text{radius}} = \\dfrac{l}{r}$$". This sets "1 radian" equal to l/r for every arc; the definition is that the angle in radians is l/r, and one radian is the case l = r. Correction: "$$\\theta \\text{ (in radians)} = \\dfrac{\\text{arc length}}{\\text{radius}} = \\dfrac{l}{r}, \\text{ so one radian is the angle where } l = r.$$" (The lesson\'s summary line "$\\theta = \\frac{l}{r}$" is already right.)',
